WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.073
- Hello, it looks like we are live.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.970
Hello everyone, Sal
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.180
Welcome to the daily homeroom

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.570
for those of y'all that
this is the first time

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:15.790
that you're joining.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:19.290
This is really a way
to connect and realize

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.080
that we're all part of
a global community here,

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.210
especially in this time
of school closures.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:25.260
Obviously Khan Academy
we're a not-for-profit

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.670
mission free-world class education

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.520
for anyone anywhere.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.240
And well before this crisis

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:32.830
we're going through was happening

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:35.730
obviously our vision was how
could we support teachers,

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.860
how do we support students,

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.170
how do we support parents

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:42.290
so that any child, and any
teacher can support that child,

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.910
to go through the core
subjects from pre-K,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.290
elementary school, middle
school, high school,

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:50.430
and the core of college
across subjects and grades.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.870
I will remind folks that
we are not-for-profit

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:54.810
and so we're able to do all of this

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.910
with philanthropic support
from folks like yourself.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:59.630
And so we've always had those resources,

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:01.470
but then when we saw the
school closures happening

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.000
we realized that it's our duty,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.930
as a not-for-profit in this space,

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.500
that it's free, that it's accessible

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.090
to do whatever else we can to make sure

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:10.680
that you feel supported.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.600
So above and beyond the
many subjects and grades

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.610
that we offer in math,
English, language, arts,

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.540
science, and the
humanities on Khan Academy,

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.740
we've been trying to do these live streams

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.950
which is just a way to
connect and make announcements

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.730
and focus on specific topics

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.020
that are front-of-mind for folks

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.350
during the school closures,

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:30.940
and also things like teacher
webinars, parent webinars.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.141
We've also been experimenting
even with live class rooms

00:01:34.141 --> 00:01:36.020
at a very large scale with students

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.160
so that we can answer their questions

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.220
on topics like algebra.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.060
I do wanna give a special thanks.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.784
You know Khan Academy was already running

00:01:42.784 --> 00:01:46.040
at a little bit of a deficit
before all of this hit

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:47.680
for us to be able to do our mission

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.980
and now that our server
costs are going up two X,

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.930
and maybe approaching three X,

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:54.890
we need as much support as possible.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.940
I wanna thank folks like Bank of America

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.150
who was the first to step-up

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:02.220
followed closely by AT&amp;T,
google.org, and Nevartis.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.810
But we need more help from
corporations, from everyone,

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.420
whatever you can donate is great.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.140
So I am very excited
today about today's topic

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.690
because it's actually a
topic that many of you

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.090
have been asking about,

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.820
it's a topic that I
desperately wanna learn

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.690
about as a father of a 5-year-old

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:23.600
who in this time of social
distancing is, you know,

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.700
not to pick on him maybe
the most disruptive member

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.060
of our household right now (laughing).

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.400
And so what I'm gonna
do is I'm gonna bring on

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.240
Caroline who is actually the founder

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.940
of Khan Academy Kids and
maybe a good place to start

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.350
Caroline is, actually how did this start,

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:43.760
what is Khan Academy Kids

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.810
even before the school closures happened

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.000
and then we can talk a little bit about

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.410
what we're trying to do together

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:49.773
to support folks even more.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.260
- Sure, so Khan Academy Kids,

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.570
for those of you who don't know,

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.630
is an educational program for kids

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:59.340
ages two through seven and it includes

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.650
thousands of activities
across a variety of subjects

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.890
math, reading, language,

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:06.970
as well as social and
emotional development

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.230
and executive-function skills.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:11.420
So it's a comprehensive
program for early learning

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:14.600
for preschool through grade one

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.670
and it has a personalized learning pack

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.720
where we adapt the lessons
for each child's skill level

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.150
on specific topics.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:27.160
It also includes a library
that has hundreds of books

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.080
many of which are original
books around our characters

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:33.560
and then we also have some partnerships

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:36.510
with National Geographic,
Super Simple, and Bellwether

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.520
to deliver some of the
other books and content.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.470
- And as a reminder this is all free,

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:43.470
all non-commercial and
I wanna give Caroline,

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.660
and her husband, Michael, credit

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:45.760
'cause they're the cool founders

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.830
of what became Khan Academy Kids.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:49.810
And I think it's important
for folks to realize

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.850
that, you know, similar to
how Khan Academy started

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:54.400
as a not-for-profit is
really a family project

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.500
a way for me to help my cousin

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.077
and word got around that
free tutoring was going on

00:03:58.077 --> 00:04:01.037
and it started being many,
everyone's cousins maybe.

00:04:01.037 --> 00:04:03.110
You started also as a family project.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:04.220
You and Michael actually created

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.770
some of the very first apps back in

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:09.900
2008, 2009, 2010,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.550
on devices that showed the world

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.020
that these can actually be
things that young children

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:14.890
can interact with.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.500
And then y'all essentially donated,

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.210
your team donated your
org to this mission.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:20.760
So I don't think I'm overstating it

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.990
that, you know, this isn't
like some corporate effort,

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:24.400
we're not trying to sell people,

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.280
with you and Michael
it's a family project,

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.620
you have other incredible team members,

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.740
it's really an act of love
that you're trying to do,

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.070
that you started for your family

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.410
and then you're trying
to do for many others.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.470
- That's right, we've been
working in early childhood

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:39.073
for 10 years on this and so, yep.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:41.663
- And your background shows that you have

00:04:41.663 --> 00:04:42.583
a much better background than I do

00:04:42.583 --> 00:04:44.140
and I need to get some

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:45.690
fingerprinting done--
- I don't know about that.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:46.880
- That might--
- Oh yes.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:47.846
- So let me,

00:04:47.846 --> 00:04:51.980
(laughing) let me start
with a couple of questions.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.173
I think, as I mentioned,

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:58.570
the early learning crowd is,

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:00.890
especially with parents working from home,

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:02.050
preschools have been closed,

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:03.603
kindergartens have been closed,

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:06.420
they are some of the
most difficult I think

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:08.340
to, you know, say, hey why
don't you just work on this

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.173
for a little bit.

00:05:09.173 --> 00:05:13.350
What advice to you have for
parents generally speaking

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.530
and potentially leveraging
Khan Academy Kids as well

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:18.023
in this time of closures.

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.670
- Yeah, I mean, I'd
say as a parent myself,

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:25.080
and Sal's a parent of
young children as well,

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.120
all I can say is, you
know, give yourself a break

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.260
and be kind to yourself to start with

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:34.020
because all of us have been unexpectedly

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:35.763
thrown into this situation.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:38.890
Already, as parents of young children,

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:41.870
we were very busy being parents

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:44.020
and juggling (mumbles) and other things

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:47.530
and now we've been catapulted
into many other roles,

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:51.500
of teacher, carer, magician,
among other things,

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.030
and there's just so much going on.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:57.300
I'd say be kind to
yourself first and foremost

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.320
and also we're in this,
you know, for the long haul

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.680
and so I think this is a new normal

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:04.130
that we're all adjusting to.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:07.640
We are working hard on some things

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:08.830
that we think might help,

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:11.610
but I think the biggest advice would be

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.680
to think of this as a balance.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.370
You're gonna be balancing some
learning time with your kids

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:20.340
as well as play time and academic time

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:25.260
and outside time, so it's
really just about the balance

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:28.340
and it's not a sprint
we're just going to have

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:30.120
to adjust to this new normal.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.040
And so a few things that we're doing

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.100
that we think may help in addition

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:38.640
to the core Khan Academy Kids program

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.290
is just like Sal is doing
these daily homerooms

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:45.540
we're launching today, daily Circle Time,

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.920
which is like Circle Time in preschools,

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:49.440
and kindergartens and first grades,

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.060
where the teachers have a chance

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:52.680
to connect with the students.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.400
So Sophie from my team and I

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.950
will be leading these Circle Times

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:59.890
and they are a time for
us to connect, read books,

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:03.550
do learning activities,
get the kids up to stretch

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.430
and do some imaginary play too.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:07.850
We're trying to keep
some semblance of what's,

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.850
of normal during these changing times.

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:13.130
And I think we're just
really acknowledging that,

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:16.150
you know, these are
difficult difficult times

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:18.050
for many families and children.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:19.860
So the children may not know

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:24.210
the specifics of what COVID-19 is,

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:25.640
but they are going through change.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.080
Maybe their school is closed,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.950
maybe their parents are working at home,

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:31.800
or their parents are just much busier

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:33.810
and more concerned about different things.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.770
So we're trying to address those head-on

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:39.030
and our first episode is a special episode

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:43.210
about how to talk to kids
through these changing times.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:46.730
And what we're doing is
instead of just explaining

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:49.170
the specifics of the virus
which they might not,

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:51.170
you know, it may not be age appropriate,

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:55.120
we are giving them three super powers

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:57.790
that they already have
that they can use tools

00:07:57.790 --> 00:07:58.880
in times of change.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:02.440
So things like, being
thankful, belly breathing,

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.053
and being a helper.

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:05.943
So in addition to

00:08:05.943 --> 00:08:06.776
Circle Time--
- Those are useful for all

00:08:06.776 --> 00:08:08.850
of us (laughing).
- I think yeah, for kids

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:10.420
of all age as well as parents.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:11.780
And so I think--
- Belly breathing.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:13.820
- As parents watching with their kids

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.210
they can do it together.

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.008
You wanna do it together (laughing)?

00:08:17.008 --> 00:08:17.841
- I love that we're doing it.
- Sal has already.

00:08:17.841 --> 00:08:19.202
(voice covers voice)
I know.

00:08:19.202 --> 00:08:20.716
- Yeah I have the belly
you was talking about

00:08:20.716 --> 00:08:21.549
is why you said to breathe.

00:08:21.549 --> 00:08:23.700
(laughing)

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.850
So let me ask, there's
a lot of great questions

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:26.790
that are already pouring in

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:27.830
and I want to get to as many of them,

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:29.300
but I'm gonna start
with a selfish question

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.400
that I think is a lot of folks are asking.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:34.940
Especially with younger ages

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:36.560
we're always sensitive to screen time,

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:38.430
and by definition Khan Academy,

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:40.660
Khan Academy Kids is on a screen.

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:43.010
But now we're in this new
normal, as you mentioned,

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:45.600
where screens are in some
ways our only lifeline

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.700
to the outside world, or socialization.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.060
How are you thinking about it,

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.330
I know you all are
consulting a lot of experts,

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:53.210
child psychologists, researchers,

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.970
what are they telling you,
what do you know about

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:57.960
appropriate use of screen time generally

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.560
and screen time in the
context of Khan Academy Kids?

00:09:01.597 --> 00:09:03.770
- Yeah, I think, as I
was alluding to earlier,

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:04.810
it's all about balance.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:07.810
So, we definitely don't
want the young kids

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:08.650
just on a screen.

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:12.640
So we need to balance a
few minutes in the morning,

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:14.903
up to 10 to 20 to 30 minutes,

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:17.530
on something like, on Academy Kids,

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:20.390
that's rich and educational and enriching

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:24.160
where you can see the kids
interacting with the technology

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:25.960
and singing and even getting up sometimes

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:28.410
to sing along and stomp around.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:31.550
But we need to balance
that with outdoor time

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:35.000
and play time, family time, free time,

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:37.060
as well as reading, reading real books.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:41.140
Or parents can also read books
in our app in the library

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:42.730
and when they do it together

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:43.930
it's a very different experience

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:47.290
than a child just looking at technology.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:49.327
So again, it's just about balance.

00:09:49.327 --> 00:09:50.700
- And just out of curiosity,

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.400
I mean we've been seeing
the traffic on our side

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:55.940
on kinda the core Khan
Academy, or non-Academy kids,

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:57.463
up about 2 1/2 fold.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:00.250
We're using this as a meeting,

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:01.690
what are y'all seeing on downloads

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:05.290
and what other information
over the last two weeks?

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:08.720
- Yeah, well overall
since our launch in 2018

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.390
Khan Academy Kids has been downloaded

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:13.870
over six million times,
has had great usage,

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:16.060
but this week is really an anomaly

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:20.270
We've seen our new users
up by over 10 times

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:21.890
our normal rates.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:22.930
So.
- Wow, wow.

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:26.870
- Very happy to be a resource
that parents and teachers

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:28.533
and families can draw upon.

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:31.560
- Right great, so let me
get to some of the questions

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.610
that I'm seeing.

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:38.250
So there's one question, my
child is on the autism spectrum

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:40.810
do you have any tips for
using the Khan Kids app

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:41.900
with children with autism?

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:44.823
How can I make sure it's
engaging and motivating for him?

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:48.520
- So we've heard from families

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:51.790
with children on the spectrum and it,

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:56.440
children are all different,
and I think our app is designed

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:59.810
to be engaging and easy
to use for all children.

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.280
So it's designed for pre-readers

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:05.440
and designed to be intuitive
for children to explore

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:09.040
and there's no, you
know, one way to use it.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:12.260
And I think with the core characters

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:15.140
that we have in our app
they tend to really,

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:17.010
children connect with these characters.

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:18.420
They all have different personalities

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:21.480
so different children can
latch onto different characters

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.013
and then we use a lot of
music so, as Sal was saying,

00:11:24.013 --> 00:11:27.350
this is a homegrown effort,
co-founder and husband,

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.130
plays the cello in the app

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:32.280
and we do a lot of the
music in our app ourselves

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:36.540
and all of the illustrations
and character books

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:39.490
are all done in-house by our very talented

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:40.660
animators and illustrators.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:45.660
So, yeah I think it is an
app that the pre-readers,

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:48.380
as well as children of
different abilities,

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:50.903
can use in different ways.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:53.000
- I have to say Caroline

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.270
whenever I come over to your house

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:56.740
I have a little bit of
insecurity as a parent

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:57.573
where I see your family,

00:11:57.573 --> 00:12:00.075
like, oh we should bring out
our cellos and play together.

00:12:00.075 --> 00:12:02.107
(laughing)

00:12:02.107 --> 00:12:03.487
- We do.
- There's something about,

00:12:03.487 --> 00:12:07.610
something of a role model
for me as me personally,

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:09.590
my wife does a much better job,

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:12.450
but I have aspirations.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:14.400
Let's see, we have other questions.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:18.210
So this is, this is from YouTube.

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:21.125
Krehenge wants to know how would you use

00:12:21.125 --> 00:12:23.320
Khan Academy Kids if there isn't

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:25.520
a translation for their language yet?

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:27.040
And I have some ideas
there but I'm curious

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:28.950
to what you think Caroline.

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:31.247
- Yeah well we've heard from
families from all of the world

00:12:31.247 --> 00:12:34.540
that they're using it to
learn English, is one thing,

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:35.740
but again as I was saying,

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:38.730
it's been designed for pre-readers.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:43.070
So children can play with
the interactive activities,

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:46.470
do the counting, and the logic as well as,

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:48.230
you know, listen to the books

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:50.370
that are read aloud in English

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:52.170
even if they don't know and I think

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:54.410
kids learn really quickly

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:57.890
so that's one way we've
seen it being used.

00:12:57.890 --> 00:13:00.070
- Yeah, exactly because
it's literally at a level

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:02.500
for when people are literally
just learning language

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.830
and numbers and motor skills,

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:05.880
and social/emotional learning,

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:08.430
that actually almost a child anywhere

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:09.860
can actually interact with it.

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:11.330
And in many parts of the world

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:12.440
it's actually a really useful skill

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:13.340
to actually have some of that

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:14.920
baseline English proficiency,

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:16.660
but, you know, longer term
we might be able to think

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:19.630
about ways to localize.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:21.480
All right, so other questions.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.050
So let's see from Facebook,
Andre Philipe says,

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:24.920
I was wondering if Khan Academy Kids

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:26.950
has a course on how to teach online?

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:28.560
Of course everything is explained so well,

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:29.930
well you're being very generous Andre,

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:31.360
and clearly because of COVID-19

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:32.420
I'm forced to teach online.

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:35.053
So, I think I can handle that question.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:38.230
We don't have any courses on that yet.

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:40.540
My advice to you, and this goes back to

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:42.090
when I was tutoring for my cousins,

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:42.923
and that was really a blessing

00:13:42.923 --> 00:13:43.920
that I started with my cousins

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:45.130
'cause it allowed me not

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:46.440
to put too much pressure on myself,

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:49.640
is just get started, there's
many ways you can do it.

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:51.690
You can use your cellphone,
use a whiteboard,

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:54.150
you can find some, you
know, you can go online

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:57.220
and buy a camera,

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:59.310
a phone holder stand,

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:00.400
that you can point downwards

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:02.540
and you can do lectures on paper.

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:04.750
You obviously can also do what I do

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:06.960
which is a digital, I use an art program,

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:08.510
I use SketchBook and you can,

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:10.330
I do screen capture using Camtasia

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:12.310
and I have a decent microphone.

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:13.560
This is something you can buy

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:16.410
at your local electronic store

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:17.470
and you could do it that way.

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:19.210
But that's the technical setup

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:21.290
and my advice on how to do it is just,

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:22.660
you know, be yourself.

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:24.320
Don't, sometimes when people get recorded

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:25.820
they can freeze up a little bit,

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:28.030
or get a little stressed out, be yourself,

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:29.910
laugh at yourself, feel like,

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:31.240
these are your students you're talking to,

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:32.280
feel like you're talking to them,

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:33.750
feel like you're talking to your cousins.

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:36.330
And I think the more you just get started,

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:37.870
get a lot of feedback from them,

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.030
keep iterating on it, I
think you're gonna develop

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:42.290
your own style and it'll be pretty cool.

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:46.020
See other questions, from Facebook,

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.310
Trixie Hansen says, can
you guys do a live tutoring

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:50.820
on YouTube, Facebook for
students if they need help

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:52.860
only using their Khan Academy work?

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.610
And then there's a second part.

00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:57.290
Why can't my parents print a report card

00:14:57.290 --> 00:14:59.840
if all of my progress and
assignment scores online?

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:05.840
So, Trixie, we actually are
exploring some of those things

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:07.660
like a YouTube, we've actually
been doing these webinars.

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:10.100
We've been publicizing it on social media

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:13.780
and on the link on that daily
schedule that we put out

00:15:13.780 --> 00:15:16.030
which are schedules for
students of different age groups

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:18.020
including the Khan Academy Kids crowd,

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:19.250
to structure their days.

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:21.410
We've been saying, we've
running some experiments

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:22.780
and starting with algebra.

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:24.740
We've been working though
algebra-worked examples,

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:26.900
but we're making them as
interactive as possible

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:29.350
where students can answer the questions,

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:31.810
you know, answer surveys,
we can see what they did

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:33.060
and then we can answer their questions.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:35.880
So expect to see some more of that,

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:37.150
we're trying to do as much as we can.

00:15:37.150 --> 00:15:40.610
And print your report card of progress,

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:41.443
in theory they can.

00:15:41.443 --> 00:15:44.480
You know we haven't built
a specific functions for,

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.900
I guess you could say formal
printable report cards,

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:49.740
but the teachers and parent dashboards

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:53.700
have reports on, if they
are a student's coach,

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:55.820
what the student has been working on,

00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:57.250
what they've gotten mastery in,

00:15:57.250 --> 00:15:58.770
what assignments they've completed,

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:03.040
so in theory that could
be printed, printed out.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:05.970
So from YouTube, Anusha Amed asks,

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:09.453
can you use Khan Academy
Kids on a computer, Caroline?

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:13.640
- Unfortunately not yet.

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:16.990
It's more for mobile
devices on IOS and Android

00:16:16.990 --> 00:16:18.820
as well as Chromebooks.

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:20.600
But we, with the Circle Time

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:22.610
you'll be able to see that on YouTube.

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:25.880
We also are coming out with
daily offline activities

00:16:25.880 --> 00:16:30.600
where children can do
math and reading offline

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:34.580
in printable or using
things around their house.

00:16:34.580 --> 00:16:35.957
So things like "Math Mondays,"

00:16:35.957 --> 00:16:37.260
"Writing Workshop Wednesdays,"

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:39.430
and "Thoughtful Thursdays."

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:40.990
- And just to add to what Caroline said,

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:44.510
you know, this is all
free, not-for-profit,

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:46.760
so whether you're on Android or IOS

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:48.120
you can download this app,

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:51.540
there's no shady upselling,
this is literally free,

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:52.850
philanthropically supported,

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:56.570
so we need your help to
keep Khan Academy Kids going

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:59.850
especially with the load we're
seeing 10 X of normal load.

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:01.760
We already had six million
downloads before this

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:03.410
and now we're at 10 X of normal load

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:04.490
so we need your help.

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:07.630
But this is all free,
this is all noncommercial

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:09.390
so hopefully it's accessible

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:11.870
to you and your children Anusha.

00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:15.840
So, from YouTube we have Astro Jayden.

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:17.570
Says, hey Sal this is Jayden I'm wondering

00:17:17.570 --> 00:17:19.860
if I can change up my
schedule to fit my day.

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:21.700
I'm in 6th grade and there's no telling

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:23.850
how each day will go?

00:17:23.850 --> 00:17:28.640
And my answer to you, or
response Astro, is absolutely.

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:30.700
The whole goal of those schedules

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:32.290
are to be a starting point.

00:17:32.290 --> 00:17:34.510
Allow people to realize, okay, I got this,

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:36.540
this is a reasonable way to handle it.

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:39.610
But feel free to copy
it, paste it, modify it,

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:41.360
so it meets your needs

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:42.650
so that it can meet your day.

00:17:42.650 --> 00:17:44.240
And I think like its
sounds like you do want

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:45.770
to have some type of a schedule

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:47.160
which does help structure your days

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:50.240
especially when we're all
spending a lot of time at home

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:51.390
which I think is a really great thing.

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:53.610
And I think that's actually
a great skill as 6th grader

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:55.040
that you're developing there's this like,

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:57.440
that meta-skill, okay how
should I structure my day?

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:58.490
How should I plan?

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:00.060
How should I make sure
I get everything done

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:00.893
that I want to?

00:18:00.893 --> 00:18:03.230
So, good job Astro.

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:07.540
Okay, another question
and this is from YouTube,

00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:08.373
littlegummy34.

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:13.570
Hi Sal, why are missions
going down on Khan Academy?

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:15.760
All right that's a very good question

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:17.800
that I'm sure many people are asking.

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:19.370
Missions is actually,

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:20.900
so for those of you who don't know,

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:23.010
historically over the last 10 years

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:24.890
there's really been two ways
to experience Khan Academy,

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:26.250
really three ways.

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:28.773
One is you do a web search on a concept,

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:31.860
factoring quadratics, and then you get,

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:33.500
go to the right part of Khan Academy

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:34.410
and you could do it that way,

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:36.580
we've historically called
that the library review,

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:38.560
and then there's this
thing called missions

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:41.470
where if you find the
missions on say algebra,

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:45.660
or other subjects it will
be kind of a guided practice

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:48.290
and there's game mechanics and
all this notions of mastery.

00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:51.670
And what we realized about
four or five years ago

00:18:51.670 --> 00:18:53.150
is missions actually has a lot

00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:55.030
of efficacy research behind it,

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:56.600
but we realized it was kind of unfortunate

00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:57.830
that there was these two worlds.

00:18:57.830 --> 00:19:00.450
Our ideal world is to do
what a good tutor would do

00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:02.450
is that you come to us for help,

00:19:02.450 --> 00:19:04.530
through a web search, and then we help you

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:06.820
for your immediate need,
maybe you're trying

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:08.160
to learn something for a test tomorrow,

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.850
or do your homework, but
then the game mechanics

00:19:10.850 --> 00:19:13.390
of things like missions kinda suck you in,

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:14.550
in a good way.

00:19:14.550 --> 00:19:17.200
And so what we started
building about two years ago

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:18.570
is what we call course mastery.

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:22.110
So now when you do a search
and you fall into Khan Academy

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:23.540
you can get your immediate help,

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:25.050
get some practice and feedback,

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:27.890
but there's already mastery
mechanics around it.

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:30.900
And so what we, the
reason why we're going to,

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:32.770
you know, the fancy word
is deprecate missions,

00:19:32.770 --> 00:19:34.860
which means over the next year or so,

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:36.530
we're going to be taking it down,

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:39.810
is because the course
mastery is being able

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:43.520
to pick up the slack of what
missions was doing before.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:45.390
And so that's my simple answer.

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:47.960
And then we are committed
to continually improving

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:50.050
that course mastery,
so not only does it do

00:19:50.050 --> 00:19:51.220
everything that missions did,

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:53.780
we just launched things
like mastery challenges

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:56.660
on our course mastery,
but we hope it goes there

00:19:56.660 --> 00:19:59.110
and beyond and has all of the benefits

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:03.100
of our library view, but
that's an excellent question.

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:07.810
All right, so from YouTube,
Daniel Marzeta asks,

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:08.643
what is the difference between

00:20:08.643 --> 00:20:11.110
a mastery challenge and assignments?

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:13.850
Are assignments just a
focused mastery challenge,

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:14.910
is a mission mastery challenge?

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:16.330
Okay, so we're going
deep into the (mumbles)

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:17.360
but these are good questions

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:18.760
because I know there are a lot of folks,

00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:20.170
especially with the school closures,

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:21.970
spending more time at Khan Academy,

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:23.700
you're using it as the resource

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:25.370
that you're really leaning on.

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:28.880
So a mastery, so there's,
when you do skills

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:30.680
and I'll just say math,
but we have mastery

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:32.750
in other subjects as well,

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:35.300
you can focus on that
skill and it might ask you

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:36.420
to do five questions.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:39.020
And if you get four right
it'll say, you're familiar,

00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:40.650
if you get five out of five it says,

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:41.740
it'll say you're fluent.

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:44.250
And then there's another
level called mastery.

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:46.070
And the only way that you get mastery is,

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:49.630
if you're already fluent, and
you get that question right,

00:20:49.630 --> 00:20:52.690
in either a,

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:54.580
a unit test,

00:20:54.580 --> 00:20:56.970
which is sampling all of
the skills of the unit,

00:20:56.970 --> 00:21:01.620
a mastery challenge,
or a course challenge.

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:03.440
And the reason why we do that is we feel

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:05.480
that you only are truly mastering it

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:08.330
when you know when to
recognize to do that problem

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:09.920
and you're seeing it with other problems

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:12.630
and you have to kind of remember I might,

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:15.830
maybe had to apply some of
those concepts or skills.

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:17.980
Mastery challenges are
things we just launched,

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:20.300
and they always existed in missions,

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:23.730
where not only do they
allow you to up-level

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:26.790
these things that you are
already proficient in,

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:30.870
but they also are doing what's
called, spaced repetition.

00:21:30.870 --> 00:21:32.960
So we know that you can learn something,

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:34.660
we've all experienced
it, but a month later,

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:37.476
two months later, you
might have forgotten it,

00:21:37.476 --> 00:21:38.750
and especially with a lot of these skills

00:21:38.750 --> 00:21:40.860
it's really important
to retain it over time.

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:42.900
And so there's a lot of
learning science behind it,

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:45.890
but mastery challenge is,
beyond being just an opportunity

00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:48.850
to up-level the skills that
you're already proficient in,

00:21:48.850 --> 00:21:50.480
it's also a chance to make sure

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:52.960
that you still have
mastered things from before.

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:54.790
So mastery challenges tend
to be a little bit shorter,

00:21:54.790 --> 00:21:56.100
take a little less time,

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:57.490
they're good ways to level-up

00:21:57.490 --> 00:21:59.000
and they're good ways to review.

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:01.940
Unit tests are great ways
if you're just trying

00:22:01.940 --> 00:22:03.220
to get mastery in that unit,

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:04.840
you're trying to understand
what your gaps are

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:07.730
in that unit and you're
trying to level-up.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:09.810
And course challenges are great

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:11.800
if you want to understand
your understanding

00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:13.060
of the whole course,

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:15.690
maybe figure out where your gaps are

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:17.610
and then zero-in on those units

00:22:17.610 --> 00:22:19.180
that you might be having difficulty with.

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:20.840
But this is all great feedback for us

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:23.340
to just make all of this that much,

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:24.893
that much clearer.

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:28.130
Let's see, other questions we have,

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:29.390
we have a little bit more time.

00:22:29.390 --> 00:22:31.660
I'm seeing if there, if there,

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:35.743
let me ask another Khan Academy
Kids question for Caroline.

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:39.680
From YouTube, Algomarbleracer says,

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:40.640
when was the app created?

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:42.200
I'm assuming they're talking about the,

00:22:42.200 --> 00:22:43.507
we have two apps, we
have the Khan Academy app

00:22:43.507 --> 00:22:45.280
and the Khan Academy Kids app.

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:46.113
I'd love to, you know,

00:22:46.113 --> 00:22:47.590
when was the Khan Academy Kids app created

00:22:47.590 --> 00:22:49.890
and kind of where, how
do you see it evolving?

00:22:50.750 --> 00:22:55.290
- Yeah so we launched
Khan Academy kids in 2018,

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:57.300
so it's been out for a little over a year

00:22:57.300 --> 00:23:00.550
and it is rapidly evolving.

00:23:00.550 --> 00:23:04.310
We are working on more tools
to help in this environment

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:06.600
of remote learning and so stay tuned.

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:09.040
In the next few weeks we'll
have some announcements

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:12.193
about new things that we can
use for our remote learning.

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:16.240
Currently it goes from
preschool through grade one.

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:18.763
We'll be aging up to grade
two as well over time.

00:23:20.090 --> 00:23:22.930
- And, you know, I
can't, I can't overstate

00:23:22.930 --> 00:23:25.140
how powerful what Caroline, Michael,

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:26.630
and their team have created.

00:23:26.630 --> 00:23:28.690
You know you saw some videos of it,

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:31.700
but it's somewhat magical
'cause the videos kinda pause

00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:33.670
and then the kids are asked to manipulate

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:35.650
what was actually in
the video, and so it's.

00:23:35.650 --> 00:23:37.730
And I have, as I mentioned, a 5-year-old

00:23:37.730 --> 00:23:38.970
and he loves it.

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:41.240
And there's also plenty of books in there,

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:44.270
things that you can read
alongside of your children.

00:23:44.270 --> 00:23:46.370
I think you're seeing
in some of these videos,

00:23:46.370 --> 00:23:47.660
this is kinda the best use case.

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:49.870
The family gets together,
does it together,

00:23:49.870 --> 00:23:51.650
it's a time to connect.

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:53.980
And we also know there are times in this

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:55.230
where you're trying to get some work

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:57.160
and you wanna say, what's
nutritious screen time

00:23:57.160 --> 00:23:59.390
for your child where
you can feel confident

00:23:59.390 --> 00:24:00.223
they're learning.

00:24:00.223 --> 00:24:01.730
and Caroline's been doing
some incredible work

00:24:01.730 --> 00:24:03.580
with researcher's from
places like Stanford

00:24:03.580 --> 00:24:06.130
to measure the efficacy, to
constantly iterate on it.

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:08.240
So this isn't just pure entertainment

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:09.390
where it's like entertainment

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:11.720
that kinda looks kinda educational,

00:24:11.720 --> 00:24:15.130
this is real education
that happens to be engaging

00:24:15.130 --> 00:24:17.150
and really entertaining
and I think they've done a,

00:24:17.150 --> 00:24:19.300
a really incredible job at that.

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:20.590
So let's see, other questions,

00:24:20.590 --> 00:24:24.340
maybe time for, time for one question.

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:28.040
This is, so from YouTube,
Roshan Arongdale asks,

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:29.570
my sister is weak in basic math

00:24:29.570 --> 00:24:31.230
how do I help her?

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:33.830
So depending on her age one suggestion,

00:24:33.830 --> 00:24:35.620
if she's, you know, mid elementary,

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:37.890
or a little older, or late elementary,

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:39.940
or middle school age, or even high school,

00:24:39.940 --> 00:24:41.370
I actually recommend going on Khan Academy

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:42.470
and starting at the beginning.

00:24:42.470 --> 00:24:43.810
Starting on early learning,

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:45.820
which I think she'll be able
to go through quite quickly,

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:47.740
especially if she uses
things like course challenges

00:24:47.740 --> 00:24:50.240
and then go to things,

00:24:50.240 --> 00:24:52.640
try to finish the
arithmetic on Khan Academy

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:55.960
and then she could probably
engage on her grade level,

00:24:55.960 --> 00:24:56.810
depending what it is.

00:24:56.810 --> 00:24:59.310
But with school closures
the silver lining is

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:00.560
there might a little bit more time

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:02.040
to really go all the way back

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:03.530
and build those foundations.

00:25:03.530 --> 00:25:05.710
I'm confident if your sister is listening

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:08.970
tell her that it's not
that she's bad at math,

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:10.970
or not capable, or that math is hard,

00:25:10.970 --> 00:25:12.770
it's just that she probably got

00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:15.740
some gaps in her background
and then now things

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:16.740
are making a little less sense.

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:18.920
But if she goes back, fills in those gaps,

00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:19.940
not only will she find

00:25:19.940 --> 00:25:22.440
that math is fun and interesting,

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:25.640
but she can be quite good at it.

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:28.160
So I think that's all of our time today

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:30.230
and time really does go by fast

00:25:30.230 --> 00:25:31.520
when you're (laughing) having fun.

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:33.450
I wanted to thank Caroline.

00:25:33.450 --> 00:25:34.740
We're gonna post information

00:25:34.740 --> 00:25:36.380
about all of the amazing things

00:25:36.380 --> 00:25:38.860
that Caroline and her
team are doing already

00:25:38.860 --> 00:25:41.810
and the live events
they're going to be holding

00:25:41.810 --> 00:25:44.570
over the weeks and maybe months to come

00:25:44.570 --> 00:25:48.000
depending on how long this
closure situation lasts.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.610
And I just wanna remind everyone
we are a not-for-profit,

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:53.110
everything we're doing is
free, it's not commercial,

00:25:53.110 --> 00:25:55.810
it's made possible through donations

00:25:55.810 --> 00:25:59.170
from folks like yourself
and we do genuinely need

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:01.210
that help to stay afloat.

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:03.210
I always tell people our
budget is about the budget

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:05.630
of a large high school,
but we are trying to serve

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:07.670
a decent chunk of humanity right now

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:10.020
especially through this
school-closure period.

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:11.100
And Khan Academy is much more

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:13.660
than just me and Caroline,
it's over 200 folks,

00:26:13.660 --> 00:26:15.370
we have thousands of volunteers.

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:16.790
We have to spend millions of dollars

00:26:16.790 --> 00:26:17.980
just on server hosting.

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:20.450
You can imagine there's
tens of millions of people

00:26:20.450 --> 00:26:22.930
who are depending on this
every month right now.

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:25.500
So with that everyone,
you know, stay safe,

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:27.470
stay socially distanced and just remember

00:26:27.470 --> 00:26:30.010
we're all in this together and there's,

00:26:30.010 --> 00:26:31.700
it's a hard time, but
there's something beautiful

00:26:31.700 --> 00:26:34.000
about this shared experience as well

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:36.810
and I really love being connected

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:37.920
to all of y'all in this way

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:41.063
and I, and our team will,
we'll see ya tomorrow.

